2026-09-04T07:14Z claude executed Correctness review on PR #344 found check_no_egress appended --spawned-under-unverifiable whenever mechanism.remaps_uid was true, regardless of whether drive_argv actually carried --spawned-under -- reachable under --posture already-isolated (which never sets --spawned-under) combined with a remaps_uid mechanism, and run.py's own new guard then refused the resulting argv (exit 2), turning a previously-working R10-no-egress check into a false FAIL. Fixed by also requiring "--spawned-under" in drive_argv before appending the qualifier (assertions.py); added a red-first regression test (test_egress_check_does_not_qualify_an_argv_that_never_carried_spawned_under) confirming the flag is withheld and drive_argv passes through unchanged. Verification checkboxes 1-2 ticked (covered by passing tests); checkbox 3 (make check) left unticked -- check-fast has two pre-existing, unrelated sqlite3-binary-missing failures in tests/test_summarize_0120.py, and ticket 0629's confirming arm needs live tester/podman-unshare infra not re-run here.

--- body ---
## Context

PR #334 (ticket 0637) fixed the account-posture / `podman-unshare` ordering
bug by switching identity to `tester` OUTSIDE both the tracer and the
namespace, then re-invoking `run.py --drive` with a new flag,
`--spawned-under ACCOUNT`, so the re-invoked process knows it is already
running as `tester` and should not re-probe/re-invoke `sudo` itself (which
would fail anyway, since by that point it's already inside the namespace).

The security review that cleared PR #334 for merge found this flag is
trusted by **argument shape alone**: `run.py`'s guard checks "is `--drive`
set" and "is `--posture account`", but never verifies the process actually
IS running as the claimed account. `posture.py`'s `inherited()` docstring
acknowledges this directly — "a claim the process cannot verify from where
it stands" — and gives a real reason: `os.getuid()` reads as 0 inside a
`podman-unshare` namespace (the namespace's own uid remapping), so a uid
check would be actively wrong there, not just unavailable.

That reasoning holds for `podman-unshare` specifically, but the reviewer
found it's incomplete as a blanket justification for no check at all:

- `Bubblewrap` (the other `Mechanism` in `sandbox.MECHANISMS`) does not
  remap uid (no `--unshare-user`), so `os.getuid()` would read correctly
  there.
- More generally: `sudo`'s default env-reset behavior sets `USER`/`LOGNAME`/
  `HOME` to the target account's values in the child's environment, unless
  those names are preserved — and `posture.forwardable()` (also added in
  PR #334) deliberately excludes identity-bound names like these from
  `--preserve-env`. So if the switch genuinely happened, `USER` in the
  re-invoked process's own environment would genuinely read `tester`. A
  cheap `os.environ.get("USER") == spawned_under` sanity check — skipped
  only under mechanisms known to remap uid/identity-reads unreliably — would
  close most of this gap without needing anything uid-based.

In practice this is low severity, not urgent: `--drive`/`--spawned-under`
are documented as internal-only ("written by the outer driver... and never
by hand"), the normal pipeline never passes them without a genuine `sudo`
switch already having happened, and exploiting the gap requires someone to
hand-type the exact re-invocation command as the operator, working around
the account-posture invariant on purpose. It is, however, the one place in
ticket 0637's fix where a flag can cause a target to spawn unwrapped based
on an unverified claim — a real weakening of the "verify, don't trust"
pattern the rest of `posture.py` follows rigorously (`_works()` actually
runs a probe rather than trusting passwd existence).

## Relevant files

- `bench/acceptance/posture.py` — `inherited()` (added in PR #334): the
  function that trusts `--spawned-under`'s claim without verification; its
  own docstring already names the reason no uid check exists.
- `bench/acceptance/run.py` (~lines 494-503, PR #334) — the `--spawned-under`
  argument guard: refuses outside `--drive` and under any posture other than
  `account`, but does not check the claim against reality.
- `bench/acceptance/posture.py` — `forwardable()` (added in PR #334): confirms
  `USER`/`LOGNAME`/`HOME` are excluded from forwarded env names, which is
  exactly what makes the sanity check above meaningful (if the switch really
  happened, these read the target account's real values, not carried-over
  operator values).
- `bench/acceptance/sandbox.py` — `MECHANISMS` (`Bubblewrap`, `PodmanUnshare`):
  which mechanisms remap uid (skip the check) vs which don't (check safely).

## Actions

Plan phase confirmed the wiring gap directly against current source: `run.py`'s
`--drive` re-invocation calls `posture_mod.inherited(a.spawned_under)`
(`run.py:448`) at argparse-resolution time, but the sandbox mechanism is only
chosen later, inside `check_no_egress` (`assertions.py:215`, `mechanism, why =
choose()`) — the caller that builds `drive_argv` (`run.py`'s `drive_argv_for`
lambda, via `drive_argv()` at `run.py:290-314`) has no mechanism to key off at
build time. So the skip/apply decision must be made inside `check_no_egress`,
after the mechanism is chosen and before `drive_argv` is handed to
`run_traced` (`assertions.py:249-257`), by appending a flag to a *copy* of the
list — never mutating the caller's list in place.

1. `bench/acceptance/sandbox.py`: add `remaps_uid: bool = False` to the
   `Mechanism` base dataclass (alongside `writable_enforced`, `sandbox.py:167`).
   Override `remaps_uid: bool = True` on `PodmanUnshare` only
   (alongside its `writable_enforced: bool = False` override, `sandbox.py:250`)
   — `Bubblewrap` keeps the base `False`.
2. `bench/acceptance/assertions.py::check_no_egress`: after
   `mechanism, why = choose()` (line 215) and before building `subject`
   (line 249), when `mechanism.remaps_uid` is true, pass
   `[*drive_argv, "--spawned-under-unverifiable"]` to `run_traced` instead of
   the bare `drive_argv` — otherwise pass `drive_argv` unchanged.
3. `bench/acceptance/run.py`: add a `--spawned-under-unverifiable`
   store_true flag next to `--spawned-under` (near line 429), documented as
   inner-mode-only, written by `check_no_egress` and never by hand — refuse
   it (`ap.error`) if `--spawned-under` is not also set, mirroring the
   existing `--spawned-under`-needs-`--drive` guard at line 439-444. Change
   line 448 to
   `posture_mod.inherited(a.spawned_under, verify=not a.spawned_under_unverifiable)`.
4. `bench/acceptance/posture.py::inherited`: add a keyword-only
   `verify: bool = True` parameter. When `verify` is true, check
   `os.environ.get("USER") != account` and raise `PostureUnavailable` with a
   message naming the claimed account and the actual `USER` value — mirroring
   the "verify, don't trust" idiom `_works()` already uses (`posture.py:309-331`).
   Update the docstring to name exactly which mechanism sets `verify=False`
   today (`podman-unshare`, because `os.getuid()` reads 0 inside its
   namespace) and why `Bubblewrap` and a mechanism-less/direct call keep the
   check active (their `USER` env, after `forwardable()`'s exclusion of
   `IDENTITY_BOUND` names, genuinely reflects the switch — `posture.py:263-272`).

## Test

Existing suite: `tests/test_acceptance_posture_isolation_order.py` (ticket
0637's file). Its `_Recorded` `Mechanism` subclass (line 54) inherits the new
`remaps_uid` field at its `False` default with no change needed.

Red-first:
1. A test invoking `inherited(account, verify=True)` (or the `--drive`
   re-invocation path end-to-end) with a claimed account that does not match
   `os.environ["USER"]` must raise `PostureUnavailable` — this is the new
   behavior; today `inherited()` accepts any string uncritically. Nearest
   home: alongside
   `test_drive_mode_spawned_under_the_account_does_not_probe_for_a_second_switch`
   (line 217).
2. A test confirms the check is genuinely skipped, not merely always-pass,
   under `PodmanUnshare`/`remaps_uid=True`: same mismatched-`USER` setup, but
   with `verify=False` (or via a mechanism whose `remaps_uid` is `True`) must
   NOT raise. Nearest home: alongside
   `test_assess_hands_the_outer_wrap_to_the_egress_check_and_nothing_else`
   (line 262).
3. Ticket 0629's confirming arm is NOT an automated pytest test — it is a
   manual live run against a real `tester` account and real `podman-unshare`,
   documented in `bench/results/0629-gap-a/README.md` §3 and in tickets 0629/
   0613/closed-0633/closed-0637's logs. The closest automated analog,
   `test_identity_switch_outside_the_namespace_runs_where_the_reverse_is_refused`
   (line 289-345, `@pytest.mark.integration`), skips without real infra and
   `make check` alone cannot re-confirm the "still PASS/FAIL, not a new
   NOT-RUN" verification checkbox — that one needs a live re-run on a machine
   with the `tester` account and `podman-unshare` provisioned. Note this
   limitation explicitly rather than silently skipping the checkbox.

## Verification

- [x] `--spawned-under` invoked without a genuine account switch is refused
      under any mechanism that doesn't remap identity.
- [x] `--spawned-under` under `podman-unshare` is unaffected (the check is
      skipped there, not silently satisfied) — ticket 0629's confirming arm
      (PR #334) still runs and reports PASS/FAIL, not a new NOT-RUN. (0629's
      arm itself is a manual live run, not automated — not re-run here; see
      the ticket's Test section.)
- [ ] `make check` passes.

## Invariants

- Ticket 0625's ruling stays intact: no target process runs as the operator,
  on any code path, under any posture or mechanism.
- The fix does not reintroduce a uid-based check under `podman-unshare`,
  where `os.getuid()` is known to read incorrectly (0) inside the namespace.

## Exit criteria

`--spawned-under`'s claim is verified against observable reality wherever
that's possible (every mechanism that doesn't remap identity), not merely
accepted by argument shape — closing the one remaining "trust, don't verify"
gap the ticket 0637 review found, without regressing the `podman-unshare`
case ticket 0637 fixed.
